        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Customer Lifetime Value (LTV) Under Negative Churn: Solving the Geometric Series Dilemma</h2>
        <p>
          The classic textbook SaaS formula for Customer Lifetime Value assumes a static or strictly decaying revenue stream:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-indigo-300">
          LTV_traditional = (ARPU * Gross Margin %) / Customer Churn Rate
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          This formulation relies on the convergence of an infinite geometric series where the common ratio R = (1 - Churn) &lt; 1. However, when an enterprise SaaS operates with <strong>Negative Revenue Churn</strong> (e.g. -2% monthly net churn, or NRR = 102%), the ratio R &gt; 1. Substituting negative churn into the traditional formula causes the denominator to become zero or negative, resulting in a mathematically divergent infinite value (&infin;).
        </p>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 space-y-4 shadow-xl">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 font-mono">The Discounted Finite-Horizon Solution</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Institutional investors and SaaS CFOs resolve this dilemma by capping the valuation window to a realistic finite customer horizon H (typically 36 to 60 months) and applying an annual discount rate d (Weighted Average Cost of Capital, WACC ~10%–12%):
          </p>
          <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300">
            LTV_H = &sum; [ ARPU_0 * (1 - Net Churn)^t * Gross Margin % ] / [ (1 + d_monthly)^t ] for t=1 to H
          </div>

