        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>What is the difference between Customer (Logo) Churn and Revenue Churn?</span>
              <span class="text-indigo-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Customer (logo) churn measures the percentage of customer accounts that cancel during a given timeframe. Revenue churn measures the percentage of Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR) lost through cancellations and account downgrades. In businesses with tiered pricing, a company can lose many low-tier customers while maintaining or growing revenue if high-tier accounts expand.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>What is Negative Revenue Churn and how does it occur?</span>
              <span class="text-indigo-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Negative revenue churn occurs when expansion revenue (upsells, seat additions, cross-sells, usage growth) and reactivation revenue from existing customers exceeds the total revenue lost to cancellations and contractions. When net revenue churn is negative, a SaaS business grows in total MRR each month even with zero new customer acquisition.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>What is the formula for Gross MRR Churn vs Net MRR Churn?</span>
              <span class="text-indigo-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Gross MRR Churn % = [(Churned MRR + Contraction MRR) / Starting MRR] × 100. It measures raw loss and is strictly non-negative. Net MRR Churn % = [((Churned MRR + Contraction MRR) - (Expansion MRR + Reactivation MRR)) / Starting MRR] × 100. It can be negative when expansion outpaces loss.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>How does Net Revenue Churn relate to Net Revenue Retention (NRR)?</span>
              <span class="text-indigo-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Net Revenue Churn and Net Revenue Retention (NRR) are inverse expressions of the exact same economic reality: NRR % = 100% - Net Revenue Churn %. A monthly net revenue churn of -2% equates to a monthly NRR of 102%, which compounds to approximately 126.8% annualized NRR.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>Why do venture capitalists and acquirers prioritize Revenue Churn over Logo Churn?</span>
              <span class="text-indigo-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Revenue churn directly dictates customer lifetime value (LTV), capital efficiency, and valuation multiples. A SaaS with high logo churn in its self-serve tier can still generate an industry-leading Net Expansion Rate from mid-market and enterprise cohorts, resulting in durable compound growth that justifies 10x+ ARR valuation multiples.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

